course: books
lesson: 80
title: Just So Stories (Rudyard Kipling), 5: The Elephant's Child, part 2 (the tug-of-war and the trunk)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Python-Rock-Snake tells the Elephant's Child to pull for his life, and a huge tug-of-war begins: the Crocodile pulls backwards into the river and the little elephant pulls back, and his nose stretches and stretches.
explain: The Snake wraps himself round the Elephant's Child's back legs and pulls too, and at last the Crocodile lets go with a "plop" heard all along the Limpopo; the Elephant's Child says thank you and cools his sore nose in the river, waiting for it to shrink.
explain: It never shrinks: it has become a real trunk, and soon he finds it can swat flies, pull up grass and slap cool mud on his head, three "'Vantages" the Snake points out.
explain: He walks home happy, using his trunk for everything and even picking up the melon rind he dropped, but at home he spanks his whole family back, until they all rush off to the Crocodile to get trunks of their own, and after that nobody spanks anybody.
explain: The poem after the story praises questions: Kipling's "six honest serving-men" are What, Where, When, How, Why and Who, and a small curious child keeps millions of them busy.
fact: The Python told him to "pull as hard as ever you can" or the Crocodile, "your acquaintance in the large-pattern leather ulster", would jerk him into the river "before you can say Jack Robinson" (very quickly); an "ulster" is a long overcoat.
fact: The Elephant's Child "sat back on his little haunches, and pulled, and pulled, and pulled, and his nose began to stretch", while the Crocodile "threshed his tail like an oar"; "and it hurt him hijjus!" (hideously, terribly).
fact: His nose became "nearly five feet long", and he said "This is too butch for be!" (This is too much for me!).
fact: The Snake "knotted himself in a double-clove-hitch" (a sailor's knot) round his hind legs, calling the Crocodile a "self-propelling man-of-war with the armour-plated upper deck" (a warship with armour on top), "That is the way all Bi-Coloured-Python-Rock-Snakes always talk."
fact: "At last the Crocodile let go of the Elephant's Child's nose with a plop that you could hear all up and down the Limpopo."
fact: He first said "Thank you" to the Snake, then wrapped his nose in cool banana leaves and hung it in the river; the Snake said "Some people do not know what is good for them."
fact: He waited three days for it to shrink, but "it never grew any shorter, and, besides, it made him squint"; it was "a really truly trunk same as all Elephants have to-day".
fact: "'Vantage" is short for "advantage" (a good point): one, he swatted a fly dead; two, he plucked grass, dusted it clean against his fore-legs and ate it; three, he "schlooped up a schloop of mud" and made a cool mud-cap.
fact: Going home he pulled fruit down from trees, used a branch as a fly-whisk, and sang down his trunk "louder than several brass bands"; he picked up the melon rind he had dropped, "for he was a Tidy Pachyderm" (a thick-skinned animal). On the way he also went out of his way to spank a Hippopotamus who was no relation of his, to make sure the Snake had told the truth about his new trunk.
fact: At home he knocked two brothers "head over heels", threw the Baboon (who said the trunk "looks very ugly") into a hornets' nest, pulled the Ostrich's tail-feathers, dragged the Giraffe through a thorn-bush, and blew bubbles in the Hippo's ear, "but he never let any one touch Kolokolo Bird".
fact: His families went "to borrow new noses from the Crocodile. When they came back nobody spanked anybody any more"; hitting people is wrong, and the story's ending is happiest when nobody does it.
fact: The poem: "I keep six honest serving-men ... Their names are What and Where and When / And How and Why and Who"; a small girl keeps "One million Hows, two million Wheres, / And seven million Whys!" The poet lets his six "rest from nine till five, / For I am busy then, / As well as breakfast, lunch, and tea, / For they are hungry men."
fact: Real biology: a trunk is the nose and upper lip joined together, with no bones and many thousands of muscle bundles, so it can lift a log or pick up a single peanut.
fact: Real biology: elephants smell, breathe, touch, trumpet, and spray dust and mud with their trunks; to drink they suck water a little way up the trunk and squirt it into their mouths, not drink through it like a straw.
fact: Real biology: elephants got trunks slowly over millions of years, as their ancestors changed from generation to generation; no nose was ever stretched by a crocodile, and a stretched body part is not passed on to babies.
exercise: Who told the Elephant's Child to pull as hard as ever he could? | The Bi-Coloured-Python-Rock-Snake | The Kolokolo Bird | His hairy uncle, the Baboon, from the trees | The Crocodile himself
exercise: What did the Snake call the Crocodile first? | "Your acquaintance in the large-pattern leather ulster" | "A self-propelling log of wood with very sharp teeth" | "Noble and generous Cetacean of the Limpopo" | "My long and bubbling friend in the green water"
exercise: What is an "ulster"? | A long overcoat | A kind of river boat | A big river fish | A knot sailors tie
exercise: What does "before you can say Jack Robinson" mean? | Very quickly | Very, very slowly | In a loud voice | After dinner-time
exercise: What happened to the Elephant's Child's nose as they pulled? | It stretched longer and longer | It went flat and wide, like a pancake | It turned bright red | It came right off
exercise: How long was his nose when he felt his legs slipping? | Nearly five feet long | About seventeen feet, like a Giraffe | As big as a boot | Ten miles long
exercise: What did "This is too butch for be!" mean? | This is too much for me! | This is too butch for a bee! | This is too big to be! | This is too much fun for me!
exercise: How did the Snake help in the tug-of-war? | Knotted himself round the hind legs and pulled | Bit the Crocodile hard on the end of his tail | Called all the aunts and uncles to come and pull | Threw a big rock at the Crocodile's armoured back
exercise: What did the Snake call the Crocodile the second time? | A self-propelling man-of-war with the armour-plated upper deck | A large-pattern leather ulster with a great many sharp teeth | A big log of wood lying at the edge of the greasy river | A noble and generous Cetacean who has eaten every fish
exercise: How did the Crocodile let go? | With a plop you could hear all up and down the Limpopo | With a crash that shook all the fever-trees by the river | Quietly, while the Snake was talking in his long words | With a howl that woke the Kolokolo Bird in her thorn-bush
exercise: What was the first thing the Elephant's Child did after he sat down? | Said "Thank you" to the Snake | Spanked the Crocodile hard | Ran home as fast as he could | Ate seventeen melons in a row
exercise: Why did he hang his nose in the river? | He was waiting for it to shrink | He was trying to catch fish with it | He wanted to wash off the mud | He was drinking the river water
exercise: How long did he wait for his nose to shrink? | Three days | Three weeks | Five whole weeks | Just one night
exercise: What else did the long nose do to him while he waited? | Made him squint | Made him sneeze a lot | Made him very sleepy | Made him very hungry
exercise: What was 'Vantage number one? | He hit a fly dead with the end of his trunk | He picked a bundle of grass and ate it | He made a cool mud-cap for his hot head | He spanked a broad Hippopotamus very hard
exercise: How did he clean the grass before eating it? | Dusted it against his fore-legs | Washed it in the Limpopo River | Shook it in the air three times | Blew on it hard with his trunk
exercise: What was 'Vantage number three? | A cool schloopy-sloshy mud-cap | Singing louder than brass bands | Pulling fruit down from a tree | Breaking off a branch for flies
exercise: What does "'Vantage" mean? | Advantage, a good point | A kind of muddy cap | A long trunk | A way of saying thank you
exercise: How loud was his singing down his trunk? | Louder than several brass bands | Quieter than a little Kolokolo Bird | As loud as a howling desert | Louder than a hundred Djinns
exercise: What did he do with the melon rind on the way home? | Picked it up, for he was a Tidy Pachyderm | Threw it at the Python-Rock-Snake for fun | Gave it all to the Crocodile for his dinner | Left it there for the birds to eat up
exercise: What is a "pachyderm"? | A thick-skinned animal | A very tidy person | A kind of melon | A long thin snake from the river
exercise: What did his hairy uncle the Baboon say about the trunk? | "It looks very ugly" | "It looks very useful" | "Where did you get it?" | "Let me try it on"
exercise: Whom did he never let anyone touch? | Kolokolo Bird | The Python-Rock-Snake | The old Crocodile | His own mother
exercise: How did the story end for the whole family? | They got trunks from the Crocodile, and nobody spanked any more | They all ran away from the Elephant's Child into the forest | They spanked the Elephant's Child even harder than ever before | They asked the Djinn of All Deserts to take the trunk away again
exercise: Name the six honest serving-men in the poem. | What, Where, When, How, Why and Who | Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday and the rest | Horse, Dog, Ox, Man, Camel and Djinn | One, two, three, four, five and six
exercise: Real biology: what is an elephant's trunk made of? | The nose and upper lip, with no bones and many muscles | The nose alone, held stiff by one long and narrow bone | The upper lip only, with a row of small teeth inside | A tube of horn, like a rhino's horn but very long
exercise: Real biology: how does an elephant drink? | It sucks water up its trunk and squirts it into its mouth | It drinks straight up through its trunk like a long, bendy straw | It kneels and laps the water with its long tongue | It never drinks, but gets all its water from grass
exercise: Real biology: how did elephants really get their trunks? | Slowly, over millions of years | From a tug-of-war with a crocodile | By stretching them a little each day | From a Djinn's magic in the desert
test: Harry says: "The Elephant's Child pulled and the Crocodile let go cos he got bored." Put him right. | The Elephant's Child and the Snake pulled hardest | The Kolokolo Bird flew down and pecked the Crocodile's eye | The Crocodile's teeth fell out from pulling | The family arrived and pulled him free
test: Priya, India: "Kindly do the needful and tell what is 'Vantage number two." ("do the needful" = do what is needed.) | Plucking grass and eating it | Swatting a fly | Making a mud-cap | Spanking someone
test: What would have happened if the Snake had not helped? | The Crocodile might have pulled him into the river | The nose would have shrunk back to a boot-size | The Crocodile would have told him his dinner | He would have been home three days earlier
test: Lucy, Gen Z: "Elephant's Child really went home and threw hands with his whole fam, chaotic energy." ("threw hands" = started hitting, "fam" = family.) Why did his family stop spanking in the end? | Everyone got a trunk from the Crocodile | The Kolokolo Bird told them all to stop | The Python came and frightened them all | They grew too tired to spank any more
test: True or false: the Python-Rock-Snake was only ever nasty to the Elephant's Child. | False: he spanked him once but then saved him and taught him | True: he spanked him and laughed when the Crocodile grabbed him | True: he only came down the bank to watch him being eaten | False: he never spanked him at all, he was kind from the start
test: Ahmed says: "Elephants stretched their noses a bit each day and passed it on to their babies, innit." Put him right. | Trunks grew over millions of years as each generation was born | Yes, that is exactly how all elephant babies get long noses | No, the Crocodile still pulls each baby elephant's nose today | No, baby elephants are born with no nose and grow one later
test: Grandpa, Yorkshire: "Uncle Baboon said it were right ugly, and then got chucked int' hornets' nest, serves 'im right, eh?" What do we think about the Elephant's Child hitting back? | It's a silly joke, but hitting back is not a good way | It was right, everyone should hit back much harder | The Baboon deserved the hornets for being hairy | It was the only way to get a trunk from the Croc
test: Why did the Elephant's Child go "out of his way" to spank a Hippopotamus? | To check if the Snake was right about his trunk | Because she had spanked him the hardest of all at home | Because she was a close relation of his mother | To get her to come with him to the Crocodile
test: Compare the Elephant's Child at the start and at the end. Which is true? | He starts trusting and spanked; he ends strong and a bit of a bully | He starts rude and greedy; he ends polite and gentle to everyone | He starts with a trunk; he ends with a nose as big as a boot | He starts brave and loud; he ends shy and afraid of all his family
test: In the Leopard story the animals' new looks came from hiding in shade; in this story the trunk came from a tug-of-war. In which earlier story did a helper tie something with a knot or a strap? | The Whale: the Mariner tied the grating with his suspenders | The Camel: the Djinn tied the hump on with his dust-cloak | The Rhino: the Parsee tied the skin up with three buttons | The Leopard: Baviaan tied the Zebra's stripes with a rope
test: Like the Camel's hump, the trunk at first looks like trouble. What do both turn out to be? | Useful: the hump stores food, the trunk does many jobs | Useless: both are only ugly lumps to laugh at | Punishments that never helped them in any way | Gifts from a Djinn who lives in a desert
test: Ben, Canada: "Crocodile's a total hoser, eh, but the Python's a beauty." ("hoser" = idiot, "beauty" = a great person.) Who said "Some people do not know what is good for them"? | The Python | The Crocodile | The Kolokolo Bird | The hairy Baboon
test: Older cousin: "Kipling says ten million serving-men, seven million Whys. That's her, our Ellie, never stops asking." What are the "serving-men" really? | Question words | Grown-up servants | Little soldiers | Melon seeds
test: ★★★ In the poem the small girl keeps one million Hows, two million Wheres and seven million Whys. How many is that? | Ten million | Nine million | Seven million | Three million
test: ★★★ The poem says the six serving-men rest "from nine till five", "As well as breakfast, lunch, and tea". Trap: why does the poet rest them then? | He is busy then, and they are hungry men | They are tired from running east and west | The small girl is using them all day long | They have been sent abroad over land and sea
test: ★★★ Read carefully: which of these did the Elephant's Child do on his walk home, BEFORE he reached his families? | Spanked a Hippopotamus who was no relation of his | Pulled out his tall Ostrich aunt's tail-feathers | Threw his hairy uncle into a hornets' nest | Knocked two of his dear brothers head over heels
exam: Identify the creature which, by knotting itself about the hind legs of the Elephant's Child, secured his release from the Crocodile. | The Bi-Coloured-Python-Rock-Snake | The Kolokolo Bird of the thorn-bush | The broad aunt, the Hippopotamus | The tall uncle, the Giraffe
exam: Ascertain the purpose for which the Elephant's Child suspended his nose, wrapped in banana leaves, within the waters of the Limpopo. | To await its contraction to its former size | To cleanse it of the mud of the river bank | To permit the capture of fish for his dinner | To conceal it from the view of the Crocodile
exam: Select the response that enumerates correctly the three advantages of the trunk identified by the Python-Rock-Snake. | Killing a fly, plucking and eating grass, and making a mud-cap | Singing loudly, picking up rind, and spanking a Hippopotamus | Pulling down fruit, breaking branches, and coiling up snakes | Spanking brothers, pulling feathers, and blowing bubbles
exam: State, in accordance with established zoology, the anatomical composition of the trunk of an elephant. | The nose and upper lip, united and devoid of bone | The lower jaw, elongated and supported by bone | The tongue, extended and covered by thick skin | The neck, stretched and composed of vertebrae
